# Can a surprise be announced? The surprise exam paradox
tags: ["surprise exam paradox","unexpected hanging","epistemic logic","backward induction","quine"]
teaches: ["concepts/surprise-exam-paradox","concepts/epistemic-blindspots","methods/backward-induction"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed","b":"gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace","take":"lines"}
prompt: "Why does the students' proof that the exam can't happen seem airtight? Where exactly does the reasoning go wrong — and do logicians agree?"
next: {"title":"When does sand become a heap? The sorites paradox","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/paradoxes/09-when-does-sand-become-a-heap-the-sorites.cast"}

## The surprise exam paradox
sources:
    - id: chow1998
      title: The surprise examination or unexpected hanging paradox
      authors: Timothy Y. Chow
      year: 1998
      finding: 'Nearly a hundred papers published and still no consensus on the resolution; earliest variant probably Lennart Ekbom''s surprise drill; the one-day version as a Moore-type blindspot; the self-referential reading of the announcement (Amer. Math. Monthly 105: 41–51).'
      url: https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9903160
    - id: oconnor1948
      title: Pragmatic paradoxes
      authors: D. J. O'Connor
      year: 1948
      finding: First appearance of the paradox in print (Mind 57).
    - id: gardner1963
      title: 'Mathematical Games: A new paradox, and variations on it, about a man condemned to be hanged'
      authors: Martin Gardner
      year: 1963
      finding: Popularised the paradox (Scientific American, March 1963).
    - id: quine1953
      title: On a so-called paradox
      authors: W. V. O. Quine
      year: 1953
      finding: 'The elimination argument refutes only the supposition that the student knows the announcement, not the announcement itself (Mind 62: 65–67).'
    - id: kr2010
      title: The surprise examination paradox and the second incompleteness theorem
      authors: Shira Kritchman and Ran Raz
      year: 2010
      finding: 'A new proof of Gödel''s second incompleteness theorem from an argument like the surprise examination (Notices of the AMS 57: 1454–1458).'

