    card title "Can a surprise be announced?"

A: Here's a puzzle about a promise: (@draw d_mon@) can a teach (@draw d_tue@)er announce a (@draw d_wed@) surprise ex (@draw d_thu@)am, and still (@draw d_fri@) have it be a surprise?

B: Easy. Teachers do it all the time.
    draw ann

A: Say the teacher tells the class: there'll be an exam one day next week, and the night before, you won't know it's coming.
    highlight ann part "A surprise." effect underline

B: And the students, being students, set out to prove it can't happen.
    point at.ref days gesture circle

A: Their proof looks airtight. Every s (@draw q5@)tep seems innocent, yet the conclusion is plainly false, so one step must be wrong.

A: Here's our route: the students' proof, the exact step where it breaks, and why logicians still argue about the fix.
    blocking false

    highlight d_fri duration 2

    highlight d_thu duration 2

    highlight days effect box duration 3

    erase ann q5

A: The puzzle may have b (@draw h_swe@)egun in wartime Sweden, around 194 (@draw radio@)4, with a radio notice of a civil defence drill t (@highlight days effect box duration 2@)he next week, its day kept secret.

B: Let me guess. Somebody proved the drill couldn't happen.
    highlight days

A: A math (@highlight h_swe duration 2@)s teacher, Lennart Ekbom, is said to have spotted it. It reached print in 1948, and Ma (@draw h_48 ends@)rtin Gardner made it famous in 19 (@draw h_63 ends@)63.

    clear keep ["days","h_swe","radio","h_48","h_63"]

    card title "The students' proof"

A: The studen (@erase h_swe radio h_48 h_63@)ts start at the end of the week. Suppose Monday to  (@draw moon@)Thursday pass, and there's been no exam.

B: Then it has to be Friday, and we'd know that on Thursday night.
    highlight d_fri

    ask "Which day can the students rule out first? Tap it." choose ["d_mon","d_tue","d_wed","d_thu","d_fri"] answer d_fri store first voice b
        right: Friday: by Thursday night, a Friday exam would be no surprise.
        wrong: Picture Thursday night with no exam yet: what is left?

A: So Friday i (@draw x_fri@)s out. A Friday exam couldn't be a surprise.

A: Now Thursday. Friday's gone, so if We (@move moon by [-190,0] duration 0.8@)dnesday passes with no exam, Thursda (@highlight d_thu duration 1.6@)y is the only day left, and they'd see it comin (@draw x_thu ends@)g.

B: Sam (@move moon by [-190,0] duration 0.6@)e again  (@draw x_wed@)for Wednesd (@move moon by [-190,0] duration 0.6@)ay, the (@draw x_tue@)n Tuesda (@erase moon@)y, th (@draw x_mon@)en Monday.

A: Five  (@highlight crosses duration 1.5@)days, five crosses. A (@draw none@) surprise exam is impossible, the students conclude, and they stop revising.

B: Then on Wednesday (@draw exam@) morning, the teacher hands out the exam.

A: And nobod (@draw box_wed@)y saw it coming.  (@draw none_x@)The teacher said exactly what would happen, and it happened.

B: So the proof was wrong. But which step? Each one looked fine to me.
    highlight crosses

    clear

    card title "Where it breaks"

A: Shrink the week to a single day. The teacher says: the (@draw s1@)re's an exam tomorrow, an (@draw s2@)d you don't know it.

B: Well, now I do know it. You just told me.
    highlight s2

A: That's the trap. If  (@draw s1_ok@)you believe the first half (@draw s2_x s2_f@), the second half turns false. The sentence ca (@highlight s1 s2 duration 2.5@)n be true, but you can never know it is.

A: It's like sa (@draw rain@)ying, it's raining, but you don't know it. I can say that trul (@highlight rain part "you don't know it" effect underline@)y about you; you can never know it about yourself.

B: A sentence that works for  (@draw blind@)everyone except the person it's about.

    erase s1 s2 s1_ok s2_x s2_f rain blind

A: Philo (@draw days@)sophers call t (@draw lcross parallel true@)hat a blindspot. And the week has one:  (@draw moon2@)Thursday night, with onl (@draw blind2@)y Friday left.

A: By then the announcement has sh (@draw e_fri@)runk to exactly (@draw b_fri@) our sentence: an exam on Friday, and you don't know it.

B: Which they can hear, but never know.
    draw or e_wrong b_wrong

A: So all they can say on Thursday night is: Friday, o (@draw notk@)r the teacher was wrong. That isn't knowing it's Friday, and thei (@point at.ref d_fri gesture circle@)r first cross assumed it was.

A: That's Willard Quine's diagnosis, from 1953: the proof shows the students can't know the announcement, not that it's false.
    draw quine

B: So the teacher can tell the truth, and the students just can't use it.
    highlight b_fri b_wrong

    erase quine

    ask "If Friday can't be ruled out, whose cross is in trouble next? Tap the day." choose ["d_mon","d_tue","d_wed","d_thu"] answer d_thu store next after clear voice b
        right: Thursday: its cross leaned on Friday's.
        wrong: Each cross leaned on the one to its right.

A: Yes. And with F (@erase lx_fri@)riday not safely ruled out, Thu (@erase lx_thu@)rsday isn't either. The chain falls with  (@erase lx_wed lx_tue lx_mon@)its first link.

    clear

    card title "Do logicians agree?"

B: So it's solved. Why is it still called a paradox?
    draw solved

A: Bec (@erase solved@)ause by 1998, the mathematician Timothy Chow cou (@draw pap@)nted nearly a hundred papers on it, and stil (@draw nocon@)l no agreement on the right fix.

A: Most agree the stud (@draw c_k c_s parallel true@)ents slip somewhere. They disagree about where to point.

B: Point where?
    highlight c_k c_s effect box

A: One ca (@highlight c_k@)mp blames knowledge, as Quine did: the (@draw c_k2@) announcement is a blindspot, true but unknowable to the students.

A: Another blames self-reference.
    highlight c_s

A: To run the  (@draw c_s2@)proof, the announcement has to say: you can't work out the day, e (@draw loop@)ven from this very announcement.

B: A sentence about itself. We've met a few of those.
    flow loop_p loop_a duration 2

A: We have. In  (@highlight c_s duration 2@)2010, Shira Kritchman and Ran Raz turned the students' argument into a new pr (@draw godel@)oof of Gödel's second theorem.

B: So a classroom joke ends up proving theorems.
    point at.ref godel gesture underline

    clear

    card title "Can a surprise be announced?"

A: So here's where it broke: the teacher's words can be true, but on Thursday night the students can't know them, and the first c (@draw fri_q@)ross was never earned.
    draw days

    quiz "A manager announces a surprise fire drill on one of the next three days. Two days pass with no drill. What can staff honestly say that evening?" voice b
        right: Tomorrow, or the manager was wrong: they can't know which, so a drill tomorrow could still surprise them.
        wrong: Think of Thursday night: what could the students honestly say then?
        * It must be tomorrow
        + Tomorrow, or the manager was wrong
        * Tomorrow, but no surprise

B: So, can a surprise be announced? Yes. And the more airt (@draw exam box_wed@)ight your proof that it can't, the bigger the surprise.

## Where next
