    '|a||We have. In 2010, Shira Kritchman and Ran Raz turned the students'' argument into a new proof of Gödel''s second theorem.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8800
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:wmiz7h'
    '|b||So a classroom joke ends up proving theorems.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3160
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:5komwk'
    '|a||So here''s where it broke: the teacher''s words can be true, but on Thursday night the students can''t know them, and the first cross was never earned.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9840
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1thii2c'
    '|b||A manager announces a surprise fire drill on one of the next three days. Two days pass with no drill. What can staff honestly say that evening?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9400
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:hzcwr'
    '|b||Tomorrow, or the manager was wrong: they can''t know which, so a drill tomorrow could still surprise them.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6240
      voice: gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace
    '|b||So, can a surprise be announced? Yes. And the more airtight your proof that it can''t, the bigger the surprise.':
